WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.550
Film Courage:&nbsp;&nbsp;What advice do you have for
a screenwriter when they sit down to start

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:06.430
writing a screenplay?&nbsp;What are some things
they should have already done before before

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:08.880
they type that first page? Whether it’s outlining, etc.?

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:15.190
CSUN’s Eric Edson: They should have beside them a list of the

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:22.750
100 most-recent screenplays that they have
read. The published ones, the successful ones...Okay!...I’ll

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.390
give you a break...make it 50.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:33.079
You are not ready to write until you are very
well-read and well-versed in the literature

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:40.899
you are proposing to create. And in this case,
it’s screenwriting.

00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:47.920
I would say systematize your organization.
The first thing you have to do...see a really

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.789
bad habit that most screenwriters have is
that they’ve got a great idea and they’re

00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:57.300
cooking and they just start writing a screenplay
“I’m writing screenplay pages. Oh boy!”

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.889
And it’s fun but it’s going nowhere ultimately
without a plan and a very well thought out

00:01:02.889 --> 00:01:09.410
one. The hard work is in the outline and it
has to be a very complete outline and you

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:15.030
have to have confidence that all story problems
have been addressed.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:21.670
So the first thing you have to do is the dirty
work, the outline. Write yourself...I would

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:26.670
say hero-goal sequences because I happen to
think and I happen to know that it works.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:34.679
But however you know, beat sheet, (however
they do it), have a very complete outline.

00:01:34.679 --> 00:01:36.590
And then good bloody luck.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:47.060
It’s a lot of work and then I would also
say...yeah, you can write it on a little piece

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:54.700
of paper and tape it above your desk, “Write
Badly With Pride.” No writer’s block.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:00.049
None. Just write badly with pride. Write a
piece of junk.

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:05.660
You know, if you haven’t, just sitting there
staring at the wall for an hour, okay. What

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:11.470
would you write if you were just really writing
trash, really writing garbage? Go for it.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:17.660
Because you’ll never know when the real
idea is the depth of what you are doing will

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:24.280
be sparked. You just don’t know. You don’t
know and there’s so many things you have,

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.120
the tone.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:33.480
I’m working on and beginning to have some
fun at this stage of things. I have some original

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:40.350
screenplays (of course) as it turns out, that
never sold. And frequently what happens is

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:44.520
the stuff you like the most, that’s the
stuff that doesn’t sell. And there are three

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:53.761
or four of them that I want to turn into novels
before my clock and race have been run. And

00:02:53.761 --> 00:03:01.090
I started on one some months back. And I’ve
been working a month and a half now on the

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:04.230
first five pages.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:11.480
Every day I do...I kind of fell into it, but
it’s what Ernest Hemingway did. Everyday

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:18.320
he started at page one and that’s what I
have to do by instruction of being instructed

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:24.490
by him but also by a nature, every day I start
at page one. You can do that for the first

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:29.820
40 or 50 pages you reach a point, you know
it doesn’t work anymore but what you do

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:39.300
by that, what you gain by that is do not be
in a hurry to zoom onward. Because those first

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.800
five to ten pages are the most important pages
in the whole thing, in a novel, in a screenplay.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.770
You either convince your audience it’s worth
to come along (or reader), it’s worth their

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:55.370
time to come along for this journey with you
or not. Either they trust you by then or they

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:56.800
don’t.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:07.270
So in the search for the tone, rhythm, the
use of language and character, it’s amazing

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:13.460
spending five pages with your central character
in this case. What you can learn about that

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.510
character and doing the same five pages over
and over and over and over. You’ll learn

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:25.520
a heck of a lot about your hero. This is invaluable
as you move forward. So I would say also,

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.040
don’t rush.

